      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve described by \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we can use the arc length parameterization for parametric equations \\(x(t)\\) and \\(y(t)\\). The formula for the arc length \\(L\\) is given by:\n\\[ L = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt \\]\n\nLet's solve this step-by-step:\n\n1. **Calculate the derivatives \\( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\) and \\( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\):**\n\n\\[ x(t) = 2 \\sin t \\]\n\\[ y(t) = 2 \\cos t \\]\n\nSo,\n\\[ \\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t \\]\n\\[ \\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t \\]\n\n2. **Substitute these derivatives into the arc length formula:**\n\n\\[ \\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2 = (2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2 \\]\n\\[ = 4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t \\]\n\\[ = 4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t) \\]\n\\[ = 4 \\]\n\n3. **Simplify the integrand:**\n\n\\[ \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{4} = 2 \\]\n\nSo, the arc length \\(L\\) becomes:\n\\[ L = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt \\]\n\n4. **Evaluate the integral:**\n\n\\[ L = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 1 \\, dt \\]\n\\[ L = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} \\]\n\\[ L = 2 (\\pi - 0) \\]\n\\[ L = 2\\pi \\]\n\nThus, the length of the parametric curve from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is \\(2\\pi\\).\n\nLet's confirm this using Python.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, integrate, cos, sin\n\n# Define the variable t\nt = symbols('t')\n# Derivatives of x(t) and y(t)\ndx_dt = 2 * cos(t)\ndy_dt = -2 * sin(t)\n# Arc length parameterization\nsqrt_expr = (dx_dt)**2 + (dy_dt)**2\nintegrand = sqrt_expr**(1/2)\n# Arc length integral from 0 to pi\nlength = integrate(integrand, (t, 0, symbols('pi')))\nprint(length.evalf())\n```\n```output\n6.28318530717959\n```\nThe length of the parametric curve \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\).",
