      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve \\((x,y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we need to use the formula for the arc length of a parametric curve. The formula is given by:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to find the derivatives of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) with respect to \\(t\\) and evaluate them:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\sin t) = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\cos t) = -2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to simplify the expression inside the square root:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2 = (2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2 = 4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t = 4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t) = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the formula for the arc length simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nNow, we can evaluate the integral:\n\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} dt = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2 \\pi\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the parametric curve is \\(\\boxed{2 \\pi}\\).",
