      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we start by determining the equations of the curves in Cartesian coordinates.\n\nFrom the given parametric equations:\n\\[\nx = 2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to differentiate \\(x\\) with respect to \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\sin t) = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\nNext, we differentiate \\(y\\) with respect to \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\cos t) = -2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\nThe formula for the length \\(L\\) of a parametric curve given by \\(x = f(t)\\) and \\(y = g(t)\\) from \\(t = a\\) to \\(t = b\\) is:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t\\) into the formula:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSimplify the integrand:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\cos t)^2 = 4 \\cos^2 t\n\\]\n\\[\n(-2 \\sin t)^2 = 4 \\sin^2 t\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2 = 4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t\n\\]\n\nFactor out 4:\n\n\\[\n4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t = 4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)\n\\]\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\):\n\n\\[\n4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t) = 4 \\cdot 1 = 4\n\\]\n\nSo the integrand simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sqrt{4} = 2\\), we have:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nIntegrate \\(2\\) with respect to \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} dt\n\\]\n\nEvaluate the integral:\n\n\\[\n2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the parametric curve is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\pi}\n\\]",
